y and cross-Unit shared-parameter restoration, the byte-exact original IQB Aspect 2.12.3 player across its complete three-unit 17.4 booklet, and the current official Aspect 2.12.6 release against the current byte-exact Original sample Unit/Voud/Vomd graph. Legacy data-part value kinds are retained so object-valued Simple-Player state plus historical ABI's and EVA's JSON-string `allResponses`, current ABI's JSON-string `allData` response array, both DAN generations' JSON-string `all`, historical Speedtest's JSON-string `main`, and current Speedtest's JSON-string question/summary/index data parts round-trip without Player-specific rewriting; current DAN additionally retains its historical `IQBVisualUnitPlayerV2.1.0` state type and the release's embedded `3.1.0-beta` module version; current Aspect retains three distinct JSON-string data parts inside `iqb-standard@1.0` state. The gates also render all four images from the 16.17 MB Aspect Voud definition, navigate forward/backward, and confirm restoration after reload\nREADME.md:948:- original `.itcr.zip` resource packages nested in import bundles are extracted with per-entry and aggregate size limits into immutable releases, served only through participant-session resource URLs, and exposed to Verona players through `playerConfig.directDownloadUrl`; full, single-range, and bounded multi-range responses carry exact lengths, `Accept-Ranges`/`Content-Range`, `multipart/byteranges`, controlled `206`/`416` semantics, and CORS-exposed range headers. Resource preflights explicitly allow `Range`, so an originless sandboxed player can use multi-range media requests. The same pinned original package can be uploaded separately beside a loose Booklet, Unit, definition, and Player: automatic workspace dependency resolution now has a cross-store gate proving immutable five-file lineage, nested extraction, participant current-state delivery, byte-exact retrieval, and range retrieval. The browser smoke gate independently verifies the prebuilt-package path plus single- and multi-range retrieval\nREADME.md:951:- original `TimeMax` restrictions now start a server-authoritative timer on actual block entry, survive reloads and durable-store restarts, pause and resume with the run, visibly count down live in the Angular player when `unit_show_time_left` is enabled, show five-second alerts at compiled `unit_time_left_warnings` thresholds, expire into the next eligible unit, permanently close elapsed or deliberately left blocks, and enforce the original `forbidden`, `confirm`, and `allowed` leave policies for navigation and participant completion. In Demo/Review/Trial, where time restrictions are not enforced, leaving a `confirm` or `forbidden` block interrupts the timer with its exact remaining time and resumes it only on re-entry; no confirmation dialog is shown, and the current Original wording explains what the normal test flow would do. An authored `allowed` leave still cancels the timer. When a saving-mode participant returns to the Starter from a confirm-timed and leave-locked Unit, the current Original's merged dialog is shown exactly once and one approval applies both consequences. Timer lifecycle activity is auditable, monitor pause/resume/complete commands share the same state model, operators can set a validated replacement rest time for a selected timed unit without moving or resuming its run, or atomically restore a closed target timer while jumping to that block. Open-run targets plus run-detail monitoring and CSV exports expose authored limits, timer labels, states, remaining time, leave policies, and lifecycle timestamps\nREADME.md:953:- a pinned original Testcenter 14.3/15.1/15.2/17.4/17.6/18.0 compatibility corpus now imports both historical and current production-shaped packages, including both historical and current three-Booklet root Sample packages with the current two-SysCheck/expanded-roster graph, the historical 17-case and current 27-case `CY_Bklt_TC-*` Test-Controller packages